FORTH(1)                   STANDARD FORTH FUNCTIONS                    FORTH(1)NAME       forth - forth dictionarySYNOPSIS       forth ( -- )       false ( -- 0)          true ( -- -1)       boolean ( n -- flag)       not ( 32b1 -- 32b2)       and ( 32b1 32b2 -- 32b3)       or ( 32b1 32b2 -- 32b3)       xor ( 32b1 32b2 -- 32b3)       ?within ( value lower upper -- flag)       dup ( x -- x x)       drop ( x -- )       swap ( x y -- y x)             over ( x y -- x y z)       rot ( x y z -- y z x)       pick ( +n -- x)       roll ( +n -- )       ?dup ( x -- [x x] or [x])       >r ( x -- ) compilation       r> ( -- x) compilation       r@ ( -- x) compilation       depth ( -- +n)       .s ( -- )       < ( n1 n2 -- flag)       = ( w1 w2 -- flag)       > ( n1 n2 -- flag)       0< ( n -- flag)       0= ( w -- flag)       0> ( n -- flag)       u< ( u1 u2 -- flag)       nil ( -- 0)       -2 ( -- -2)       -1 ( -- -1)       0 ( -- 0)              1 ( -- 1)       2 ( -- 2)       negate ( n1 -- n2)       + ( w1 w2 -- w3)       - ( w1 w2 -- w3)       1+ ( w1 -- w2)       1- ( w1 -- w2)       2+ ( w1 -- w2)       2- ( w1 -- w2)       2* ( n1 -- n2)       2/ ( n1 -- n2)       << ( n1 n2 -- n3)       * ( w1 w2 -- w3)       um* ( u1 u2 -- u3)       um/mod ( u1 u2 -- u3 u4)       >> ( n1 n2 -- n3)       / ( w1 w2 -- w3)               mod ( n1 n2 -- n3)       /mod ( w1 w2 -- w3 w4)       */mod ( w1 w2 w3 -- w4 w5)       */ ( w1 w2 w3 -- w4)       min ( n1 n2 -- n3)       max ( n1 n2 -- n3)       abs ( n -- u)       @ ( addr -- 32b)       ! ( 23b addr -- )       w@ ( addr -- 16b)       w! ( 16b addr -- )       c@ ( addr -- 8b)       c!( 8b addr -- )       f@ ( x p w -- y)       f! ( y x p w -- z)       b@ ( x p -- y)       b! ( y x p -- z)       +! ( x1 addr -- )       cmove ( addr1 addr2 u -- )       cmove> ( addr1 addr2 u -- )       fill ( addr u 8b -- )       count ( addr1 -- addr2 +n)       -trailing ( addr +n1 -- addr +n2)       base ( -- addr)       binary ( -- )       octal ( -- )       decimal ( -- )       hex ( -- )       convert ( +d1 addr1 -- +d2 addr2)       <# ( x -- )       # ( +d1 -- +d2 )       #s ( +x -- 0)       hold ( char -- )       sign ( n -- )       #> ( x -- addr +n)       ?number ( string -- [n true] or [string false])       do ( w1 w2 -- ) immediate compilation       ?do ( w1 w2 -- ) immediate compilation       loop ( -- ) immediate compilation       +loop ( n -- ) immediate compilation       leave ( -- ) immediate compilation       i ( -- w) compilation       j ( -- w) compilation       if ( flag -- ) immediate compilation       else ( -- ) immediate compilation       then ( -- ) immediate compilation       case ( value -- ) immediate compilation       of ( value -- ) immediate compilation       endof ( -- ) immediate compilation       endcase ( -- ) immediate compilation       begin ( -- ) immediate compilation       until ( flag -- ) immediate compilation       while ( flag -- ) immediate compilation       repeat ( -- ) immediate compilation       again ( -- ) immediate compilation       recurse ( -- ) immediate compilation       tail-recurse ( -- ) immediate compilation       exit ( -- ) compilation       execute ( addr -- )       bye ( -- )       . ( n -- )       u. ( u -- )       ascii ( -- char) immediate       ." ( -- ) immediate compilation       .( ( -- ) immediate       cr ( -- )       emit ( x -- )       type ( addr +n -- )       space ( -- )       spaces ( +n -- )       key ( -- x)       expect ( addr +n -- )       span ( -- addr)       forth-83 ( -- )       quit ( -- )       abort ( -- )       abort" ( flag -- ) immediate compilation       here ( -- addr)       pad ( -- addr)       tib ( -- addr)       >body ( addr1 -- addr2)       .name ( addr1 -- )       #if ( flag -- ) immediate       #else ( -- ) immediate       #then ( -- ) immediate       #ifdef ( -- ) immediate       #ifundef ( -- ) immediate       #include ( -- )        #path ( -- )       ( ( -- ) immediate       \ ( -- ) immediate       , ( x -- )       allot ( w -- )       does> ( -- ) immediate compilation       immediate ( -- )       execution ( -- )               compilation ( -- )       private ( -- )       [compile] ( -- ) immediate compilation        ?compile ( -- ) compilation       compile ( -- ) compilation       state ( -- addr)       compiling ( -- state)       literal ( n -- ) immediate compilation       [ ( -- ) immediate       ] ( -- )       word ( char -- addr)       context ( -- addr)       current ( -- addr)       last ( -- addr)       definitions ( -- )       only ( -- )            restore ( entry -- )       ' ( -- addr)       ['] ( -- addr) immediate compilation       lookup ( addr1 vocabulary -- addr2 n)       find ( addr1 -- addr2 n)       forget ( -- )       words ( -- ) immediate       entry ( parameter mode code name -- )       forward ( -- )       : ( -- )       ; ( -- ) immediate compilation       create ( -- )       variable ( -- )       constant ( x -- )       vocabulary ( -- )       field ( x -- )DESCRIPTION       The language primitive in the Forth-83 Standard excluding the       block file word set.forth ( -- )       The name of the primary vocabulary. Execution replaces the        first vocabulary in the search order with "forth". "forth" is       initially the compilation vocabulary and the first vocabulary       in the search order. New definitions become part of the "forth"       vocabulary until a different compilation vocabulary is established.false ( -- 0)         Constant "true".true ( -- -1)       Constant "false".boolean ( n -- flag)       Maps numerical value to a boolean value, "true" or "false".       Non-zero values are mapped to "true" and zero to "false".not ( 32b1 -- 32b2)       "32b2" is the one's complements of "32b1".and ( 32b1 32b2 -- 32b3)       "32b3" is the bit-by-bit logical "and" or "32b1" and "32b2".or ( 32b1 32b2 -- 32b3)       "32b3" is the bit-by-bit inclusive-or of "32b1" with "32b2".xor ( 32b1 32b2 -- 32b3)       "32b3" is the bit-by-bit exclusive-or "32b1" with "32b2".?within ( value lower upper -- flag)       Tests if the parameter "value" is within the range "lower" to       "upper". Returns "true" if within the range else "false".dup ( x -- x x)       Duplicate "x".drop ( x -- )       "x" is removed from the stack.swap ( x y -- y x)            The top two stack entries are exchanged.over ( x y -- x y z)       "z" is a copy of "x" over "y".rot ( x y z -- y z x)       The top three stack entries are rotated, bringing the deepest       to the top.pick ( +n -- x)       "x" is a copy of the "+n"-th stack element, not counting       "+n" itself. {0..depth-1}               0 pick is equivalent to dup               1 pick is equivalent to overroll ( +n -- )       The "+n"-th stack value, not counting "+n" itself is first       removed and then transferred to the top of the stack, moving       the remaining values into the vacated position. {0..depth-1}.               0 roll is a null operation               1 roll is equivalent to swap               2 roll is equivalent to rot?dup ( x -- [x x] or [x])       Duplicate "x" if it is non-zero. >r ( x -- ) compilation       Transfer "x" to the return stack.r> ( -- x) compilation       "x" is removed from the return stack and transferred to       the data stack.r@ ( -- x) compilation       "x" is a copy of the top of the return stack.depth ( -- +n)       "+n" is the number of values contained in the data stack        before "+n" was placed on the stack..s ( -- )       Display the current stack in the format:               [ <stack depth> ] <bottom> \ .... \ <top> < ( n1 n2 -- flag)       "flag" is true if "n1" is less than "n2".= ( w1 w2 -- flag)       "flag" is true if "w1" is equal to "w2"> ( n1 n2 -- flag)       "flag" is true if "n1" is greater than "n2"0< ( n -- flag)       "flag" is true if "n" is less than zero (negative).0= ( w -- flag)       "flag" is true if "w" is zero.0> ( n -- flag)       "flag" is true if "n" is greater than zero.u< ( u1 u2 -- flag)       "flag" is true if "u1" is less than "u2".nil ( -- 0)       Constant for nil, none, pointer.-2 ( -- -2)       Constant minus two.-1 ( -- -1)       Constant minus one.0 ( -- 0)             Constant zero.  1 ( -- 1)       Constant one.       2 ( -- 2)       Constant two.+ ( w1 w2 -- w3)       "w3" is the arithmetic sum of "w1" and "w2".- ( w1 w2 -- w3)       "w3" is the result of subtracting "w2" from "w1".1+ ( w1 -- w2)       "w2" is the result of adding one to "w1" according to       the operation of "+".1- ( w1 -- w2)       "w2" is the result of subtracting one to "w1" according to       the operation of "-".2+ ( w1 -- w2)       "w2" is the result of adding two to "w1" according to       the operation of "+".2- ( w1 -- w2)       "w2" is the result of subtracting two to "w1" according to       the operation of "-".2* ( n1 -- n2)       "n2" is the result of arithmetically shifting "n1" left one       bit. The sign is included in the shift and remains unchanged.2/ ( n1 -- n2)       "n2" is the result of arithmetically shifting "n1" right one       bit. The sign is included in the shift and remains unchanged.<< ( n1 n2 -- n3)       "n3" is the result of logically shifting "n1" left "n2" steps.* ( w1 w2 -- w3)       "w3" is the least-significant 32 bits of the arithmetic       product of "w1" times and "w2".um* ( u1 u2 -- u3)       "u3" is the unsigned product of "u1" times "u2". All       values and arithmetic are unsigned.um/mod ( u1 u2 -- u3 u4)       "u3" is the remainder and "u4" is the floor of the quotient       after dividing "u1" by the divisor "u2". All values and       arithmetic are unsigned. An error condition results if       the divisor is zero or if the quotient lies outside the       numerical range.>> ( n1 n2 -- n3)       "n3" is the result of logically shifting "n1" right "n2" steps./ ( w1 w2 -- w3)              "w3" is the floor of the quotient of "w1" divided by the        divisor "w2". An error condition results if the divisor is        zero.mod ( n1 n2 -- n3)       "n3" is the remainder after dividing "n1" by divisor "n2".        "n3" has the same sign as "n2" or is zero. An error condition       results if the divisor is zero or if the quotient falls outside        of the numerical range./mod ( w1 w2 -- w3 w4)       "w3" is the remainder and "w4" the floor of the quotient       of "w1" divided by the divisor "w2". "w3" has the same sign       as "w2" or is zero. An error condition results if the divisor        is zero.*/mod ( w1 w2 w3 -- w4 w5)       "w1" is first multiplied by "w2" producing an intermediate        32-bit result. "w4" is the remainder and "w5" is the floor       of the quotient of the intermediate 32-bit result divided by        the divisor "w3".  A 32-bit intermediate product is used as        for */.  "w4" has the same sign as "w3" or is zero. An error       condition results if the divisor is zero.*/ ( w1 w2 w3 -- w4)       "w1" is first multiplied by "w2" producing an intermediate        32-bit result. "w4" is the floor of the quotient of the        intermediate 32-bit result divided by the divisor "w3". The        product of "w1" times "w2" is maintained as an intermediate        32-bit result for greater precision then the otherwise        equivalent sequence: w1 w2 * w3 /. An error condition results        if the divisor is zero.min ( n1 n2 -- n3)       "n3" is the lesser of "n1" and "n2" according to the operation of <. max ( n1 n2 -- n3)       "n3" is the greater of "n1" and "n2" according to the operation of >. abs ( n -- u)       "u" is the absolute value of "n". negate ( n1 -- n2)       "n2" is the two's complement of "n1", i.e., the difference        of zero less "n1".@ ( addr -- 32b)       "32b" is the value at "addr".! ( 23b addr -- )       The postfix assignment operator. Store "32b" at "addr". w@ ( addr -- 16b)       "16b" is the value at "addr".w! ( 16b addr -- )       The postfix assignment operator. Store "16b" at "addr". c@ ( addr -- 8b)       "8b" is the value at "addr".c!( 8b addr -- )       The postfix assignment operator. Store "8b" at "addr". f@ ( x p w -- y)       Field access within a 32-bit quantity. "y" are the bits within       "x" at position "p" and with a bit field width, "w". Bits positions       are counted from right to left starting with zero. A field is        defined from a position and upwards.f! ( y x p w -- z)       Inserts the value of "y" into "x" at the bit field which is defined       by the position "p" and with the width "w". The value "y" is shifted       and masked into "x" to form the result "z".b@ ( x p -- y)       Returns "true" if the bit a position "p" in "x" is one else "zero".b! ( y x p -- z)       Returns "z", the result of setting the bit at position "p" in "x"       according to the value of "y". The bit is set to zero if "y" is       zero else one.+! ( x1 addr -- )       "x1" is added to the value at "addr" using the convention       for "+". The sum replaces the original value at "addr". cmove ( addr1 addr2 u -- )       Move the "u" bytes at address "addr1" to "addr2". The byte        at "addr1" is moved first, proceeding toward high memory.        If "u" is zero nothing is moved.cmove> ( addr1 addr2 u -- )       Move the "u" bytes at address "addr1" to "addr2". The move        begins by moving the byte at ("addr1"+"u"-1) to ("addr2"+"u"-1)       and proceeds to successively lower addresses for "u" bytes.        If "u" is zero nothing is moved. Useful for sliding a string        towards higher addresses.fill ( addr u 8b -- )       "u" bytes of memory beginning at "addr" are set to "8b".        No action is taken is "u" is zero.count ( addr1 -- addr2 +n)       "addr2" is "addr1"+1 and "+n" is the length of the counted       string at "addr1". The byte "addr1" contains the byte count       "+n". Range of "+n" is {0..255}. -trailing ( addr +n1 -- addr +n2)       The character counter "+n1" of a text string beginning at       "addr" is adjusted to exclude trailing spaces. If "+n1" is       zero, then "+n2" is zero. If the entire string consists of       spaces, then "+n2" is zero. base ( -- addr)       The address of a variable containing the current numeric        conversion radix. binary ( -- )       Set the input-output numeric conversion base to 2.octal  ( -- )       Set the input-output numeric conversion base to 8.decimal        ( -- )       Set the input-output numeric conversion base to 10.hex    ( -- )       Set the input-output numeric conversion base to 16.convert        ( +d1 addr1 -- +d2 addr2)       "+d2" is the result of converting the characters within the       text beginning at "addr1"+1 into digits, using the value of       BASE, and accumulating each into "+d1" after multiplying "+d1"        by the value of "base". Conversion continues until an inconvertible       character is encountered. "addr2" is the location of the first       inconvertible character.<# ( x -- )       Initialize pictured numeric output conversion. The words:               #  #>  #s  <#  hold  sign       can be used to specify the conversion of a double number       into an ASCII text string stored in right-to-left order.# ( +d1 -- +d2 )       The remainder of "+d1" divided by the value of BASE is        converted to a ASCII character and appended to the output       string toward lower memory addresses. "+d2" if the quotient       and is maintained for further processing. Typically used       between "<#" and "#>". See: <#, #s, hold, sign and #>.#s ( +x -- 0)       "+x" is converted appending each resultant character into        the pictured numeric output string until the quotient (see: #)        is zero. A single zero is appended to the output string if the        number was initially zero. Typically used between <# and #>.        See: <#, #, hold, sign and #>.hold ( char -- )       "char" is inserted into a pictured numeric output string.        Typically used between <# and #>. See: <#, #, #S, SIGN and #>.sign ( n -- )       If "n" is negative, an ASCII "-" (minus sign) is appended       to the pictured numerical output string. Typically used       between <# and #>. #> ( x -- addr +n)       Pictured numeric output conversion is ended dropping "x".       "addr" is the address of the resulting output string.        "+n" is the number of characters in the output string.        "addr" and "+n" together are suitable for "type".        See: <#, #, #s, hold and sign.?number ( string -- [n true] or [string false])       Convert a string of character to a number using the current base.       If the conversion is not possible the string is returned with a       false flag indicating that the conversion failed otherwise the       conversion value, the number, and a true flag is returned.do ( w1 w2 -- )        immediate compilation       Used in the form:               do ... leave ...  loop       or               do ... leave ... +loop       Begins a loop which terminates based on control parameters.        The loop index begins at "w2", and terminates based on the       limit "w1". See "loop" and "+loop" for details on how the loop       is terminated. The loop is always executed at least once.?do ( w1 w2 -- ) immediate compilation       Used in the form:               ?do ... leave ...  loop       or               ?do ... leave ... +loop       Begins a checked entry loop which terminates based on control        parameters. The loop index begins at "w2", and terminates based        on the limit "w1". See "loop" and "+loop" for details on how the loop       is terminated. If "w1" and "w2" are equal the loop section is skipped.loop ( -- ) immediate compilation       Increments the "do-loop" index by one. If the new index was       incremented across the boundary between limit-1 and limit       the loop is terminated and the loop control parameters are        discarded. When the loop is not terminated, execution continues       to just after the corresponding "do". +loop ( n -- ) immediate compilation       "n" is added to the loop index. If the new index was incremented       across the boundary between limit-1 and limit then the loop is       terminated and the loop control parameters are discarded. When       the loop is not terminated, execution continues to just after        the corresponding "do". "+loop" is not available outside a colon       definition. leave ( -- ) compilation       Transfers execution to just beyond the next "loop" or "+loop".       The loop is terminated and the loop control parameters are       discarded. May only be used in the form:               do ... leave ... loop                 or               do ... leave ... +loop       "leave" may appear within other control structures which are       nested within the "do-loop" structure. More than one "leave" may       appear within a do-loop. i ( -- w) compilation       "w" is a copy of the current loop index. May only be used in       the form:               do ... i ... loop                 or               do ... i ... +loop       "i" is not visible outside a colon definition, i.e., when       text interpreting.j ( -- w) compilation       "w" is a copy of the index of the next outer loop. May only       be used within a nested "do-loop" or "do-+loop" in the form:               do ... do ... j ... loop ... loop       "j" is not visible outside a colon definition, i.e., when       text interpreting.if ( flag -- ) immediate compilation       Used in the form:               <flag> if <true-part> else <false-part> then                <flag> if <true-part> then       If "flag" is true, the words following "if" are executed       and the words following "else" until just after "then" are        skipped. The "else" part is optional. If "flag" is false,        words from "if" through "else", or from "if" through "then"       (when no "else" is used) are skipped. else ( -- ) immediate compilation       Used in the form:               flag if <true-part> else <false-part> then       else executes after the true part following "if". "else" forces       execution to continue at just after "then". then ( -- ) immediate compilation       Used in the form:               <flag> if <true-part> else <false-part> then                 or               <flag> if <true-part> then        "then" is the point where execution continues after "else",       or "if" when no "else" is present.case ( value -- ) immediate compilation       Used in the form:               case <case-structure> endcase       to mark the beginning of a case structure which should contain       a one or several:               <case-value> of <case-part> endof       The code section after the last case value part will receive "value"       as a parameter thus a default behavior is easy implemented. But       the default section may only copy this value as "endcase" is an       implicit "drop".of ( value -- ) immediate compilation       Used in the form:               <case-value> of <case-part) endof       within a case structure to define a value case.endof ( -- ) immediate compilation       Used in the form:               <case-value> of <case-part) endof       to mark the end of a cast value structure.endcase ( -- ) immediate compilation       Used in the form:               case <case-structure> endcase       to mark the end of a case structure.begin ( -- ) immediate compilation       Used in the form:               begin ... <flag> while ... repeat                 or               begin ... <flag> until                 or                begin ... again       "begin" marks the start of a word sequence for repetitive        execution. A "begin-while-repeat" loop will repeat until "flag"        is false. "begin-until" loop will be repeated until "flag" is        true and "begin-again" will repeat until "abort"-ed. The words        after "until" and "repeat" will be executed when either loop        is finished. until ( flag -- ) immediate compilation       Used in the form:               begin ... <flag> until       Marks the end of a "begin-until" loop which will terminate       based on "flag". If "flag" is true, the loop is terminated.       If "flag" is false, execution continues to just after the        corresponding "begin". while ( flag -- ) immediate compilation       Used in the form:               begin ... <flag> while ... repeat       Selects conditional execution based on "flag". When "flag"        is true, execution continues to just after the "while" through       to the "repeat" which then continues execution to just after the        "begin". When "flag" is false, execution continues to just after       the "repeat", exiting the control structure. repeat ( -- ) immediate compilation       Used in the form:               begin ... <flag> while ... repeat       At execution-time, "repeat" continues execution to just after       the corresponding "begin". again ( -- ) immediate compilation       Used in the following form to compile an eternal loop:               begin ... again       The loop construct may only be left by an "abort" or an       "exit" word in the code part of the loop.recurse ( -- ) immediate compilation       Used within a definition to make a recursive call to the       current definition.tail-recurse ( -- ) immediate compilation       Used within a definition to create a recursive call to the       current definition without saving return status. This is an       efficient way of generating iterative forms as tail recursive       calls may be performed any number of times within a definition       and corresponds to a branch to the beginning of the definition.exit ( -- ) compilation       Compiled within a colon definition such that when executed,        the colon definition returns control to the definition that        passed control to it by returning control to the return point        on the top of the return stack. An error condition exists if        the top of the return stack does not contain a valid return       point. May not be used within a "do-loop" or "do-+loop" or       an "exception"-block.execute        ( addr -- )       The word definition indicated by "addr" is executed. An       error condition exists if "addr" is not a compilation address.bye ( -- )       Leaves the interaction level and exits to the outer support       system (if any).. ( n -- )       The absolute value of "n" is displayed in a free field format       with a leading minus sign if "n" is negative. u. ( u -- )       "u" is displayed as an unsigned number in a free-field format.." ( -- ) immediate compilation       Used in the form:               ." output string"       Later execution will display the characters "output string" up        to but but including the delimiter " (close-quote). The blank        following the ." is not part of the "output string"..( ( -- ) immediate       Used in the form:               .( display comment)       The characters "display comment" up to but not including       the delimiter ")" (closing-parenthesis) are displayed. The       blank following .( is not part of the "display comment".       Recursive comments are allowed.cr ( -- )       Emits ASCII characters carriage-return and line-feed.emit ( x -- )       The least-significant 7-bit ASCII character is displayed.type ( addr +n -- )       "+n" characters are displayed from memory beginning with        the character at "addr" and continuing through consecutive        addresses. Nothing is displayed if "+n" is zero.space ( -- )       Display an ASCII space, i.e., emits an ASCII space character code.spaces ( +n -- )       Displays "+n" ASCII spaces. Nothing is displayed if "+n" is zero.key ( -- x)       The next extended ASCII character received. All valid ASCII       characters can be received. Control characters are not processed       by the system for any editing purpose. Characters received by       "key" are not displayed.expect ( addr +n -- )       Receive characters and store each into memory. The transfer       begins at "addr" proceeding towards higher addresses one byte       per character until either a "return" is received or until "+n"        characters have been transferred. No more than "+n" characters       will be stored. The "return" is not stored into memory. No        characters are received or transferred if "+n" is zero. All        characters actually received and stored into memory will be        displayed, with "return" displaying as a space. span ( -- addr)       The address of a variable containing the count of characters       actually received and stored by the last execution of "expect".forth-83 ( -- )       Assures that a FORTH-83 Standard System is available.quit ( -- )       Clears the return stack, sets interpret state, accepts new       input from the current input device, and begins text       interpretation. No messages is displayed. The text interpreter       allows casting of symbols using the vocabulary name in parenthesis       as a prefix. Example: "(string) +" may be used to specify that       the plus operator from the string vocabulary is to used. This       may occur even though this vocabulary is not in the context set.        abort ( -- )       Clears the data stack and performs the function of "quit".        No message is displayed.abort" ( flag -- ) immediate compilation       Used in the form:               <flag> abort" abort message"       When later executed, if "flag" is true the "abort message"       delimited by " (close-quote), is displayed and then a system       dependent error abort sequence, including the function "abort",        is performed. If "flag" is false, the flag is dropped and        execution continues. The blank following abort" is not part of        the "abort message". here ( -- addr)       The address of the next available dictionary location.pad ( -- addr)       The lower address of a scratch area used to hold data form       intermediate processing. The address or contents of PAD may       change and the data lost if the address of the next available        dictionary location is changed. The minimum capacity of PAD is        84 characters. tib ( -- addr)       The address of the text input buffer. This buffer is used to        hold characters when the input stream is coming from the current       input device. The minimum capacity of TIB is 256 characters.>body ( addr1 -- addr2)       "addr2" is the parameter field address corresponding to       the compilation address "addr1"..name ( addr1 -- )       Given the compilation address "add1" of an entry prints the       name of the entry.#if ( flag -- ) immediate       Used in the following form to allow conditional compilation       and execution both within and outside definitions:               <flag> #if <true-part> #else <else-part> #then       or               <flag> #if <true-part> #then       The sections are executed or compiled according to the flag       and the current mode.#else ( -- ) immediate       Marks the beginning of a "else"-part of a conditional code       section.#then ( -- ) immediate       Marks the end of a conditional code section.#ifdef ( -- ) immediate       Used in the following form for testing if a symbol already        is available:               #ifdef <name> <true-part> #else <false-part> #then       or               #ifdef <name> <true-part> #then       If <name> is available in the current search chain the true       section of code is executed or compiled according to mode       else the false section.               #ifundef ( -- ) immediate       Performs the same function as "#ifdef" but the true section is       executed if the symbol is not available in the current search       chain.#include ( -- )        Used in the following form to load source files:               #include <file name>       The file name is any string until a space character. If the       file has already been included the operation is ignored.#path ( -- )       Used in the following form to define a file search path:               #path <path name>       The input function, "#include", uses a set of path to        allow shorter file names to be used. The initial set of        paths are defined by the environment variable TILEPATH       and HOME.( ( -- ) immediate       Used in the form:               ( comment string)       The characters enclosed by the delimiter ")" are considered       comments. Comments are not otherwise processed. The blank       following "(" is not part of the comment string. The number       of characters in the comment string may be from zero to the       number of characters remaining in the input stream up to the       closing parenthesis. \ ( -- ) immediate       Used in the form:               \ comment string       Comment terminate by end of line (carriage return)., ( x -- )       Allocates space for "x" then store value at "here 4 -". allot ( w -- )       Allocated "w" bytes in the dictionary. The address to the        next available dictionary location is updated accordingly.does> ( -- ) immediate compilation       Defines the execution-time action of a word created by a        high-level defining word. Used in the form:               : <defining> <create action> does> <run-time action> ;       and then               <defining> <name>       where <create action> is "create" or any user defined word       which executes "create".       Marks the termination of the defining part of the defining       word <defining> and then begins the definition of the run-time       action for words that will later be defined by <defining>.        When <name> is later executed, the address of <name>'s parameter       field is placed on the stack and then the sequence of words       between "does>" and ";", <run-time action> are executed.        Multi-levels of high-level definitions are possible, i.e.,        "create-does>" may be used multiple times within the        <run-time action>. immediate ( -- )       Marks the most recently created dictionary entry as a word        which will be executed when encountered during compilation        rather than compiled.execution ( -- )              Marks the most recently created dictionary entry as a word        which is only available in execution mode.       compilation ( -- )       Marks the most recently created dictionary entry as a word        which is only available in compilation mode.       private ( -- )       Marks the most recently created dictionary entry as a word       which is only available in the dictionary it is created in.       The word is not available when the dictionary is not current.[compile] ( -- ) immediate compilation        Used in the form:               [compile] <name>       Forces compilation of the following word <name>. This allows       compilation of an immediate word when it would otherwise       have been executed. If <name> is not found an error message       is given.?compile ( -- ) compilation       Used as "compile" but considers the compilation state. If       compiling then performs the same action as "compile" else       does nothing.compile ( -- ) compilation       Typically used in the form:               : <name> ... compile <compiled> ... ;       When <name> is executed, the compilation address compiled for       <compiled> is compiled and not executed. <name> is typically       immediate and <compiled> is typically not immediate. state ( -- addr)       The address of a variable containing the compilation state.        True indicated compilation is occurring, false that text       interpretation is occurring. A Standard Program may not modify        this variable and should instead use "[" and "]" to alter mode. compiling ( -- state)       Returns the contents of the state variable as this variable        should not be altered by other than system functions.literal        ( n -- ) immediate compilation       Typically used in the form:               [ <value> ] literal       At execution time "n" will be left on the parameter stack.[ ( -- ) immediate       Sets interpret state. The text from the input stream is       subsequently interpreted. Typically usage see "literal". ] ( -- )       Sets compilation state. The text from the input stream       is subsequently compiled. word ( char -- addr)       Generates a counted string by non-destructively accepting       characters from the input stream until the delimiter character       code or a control character is encounter or the input stream        is exhausted. Leading delimiters and control characters are       ignored. The entire character string is stored in memory       beginning at "addr" as a sequence of bytes. The string is       ended by a null character.context ( -- addr)       Variable containing the set of vocabularies in the search chain.current ( -- addr)       Variable containing a pointer to the current vocabulary for       definitions.last ( -- addr)       Returns the compilation address of the latest defined entry       in the current vocabulary.definitions ( -- )       The compilation vocabulary, "current", is changed to be the       same as the first vocabulary in the search order, "context".only ( -- )           The compilation vocabulary, "current", is changed to be the       same as the first vocabulary in the search order, "context".       And all vocabularies except the first is removed from the        search list.restore ( entry -- )       Restores "current" to the parameter entry. Any words defined       after "entry" are unlinked form the vocabulary. This is useful       for realizing lexical levels in forth. ' ( -- addr)       Used in the form:               ' <name>       "addr" is the compilation address of <name>. Gives an error       message if <name> is not found in the current active search order.       ['] ( -- addr) immediate compilation       Used in the form:               ['] <name>       Compiles the compilation address of <name> as a literal.       When the colon definition is later executed "addr" is left       on the stack. If <name> is not found an error message is       given.lookup ( addr1 vocabulary -- addr2 n)       "addr1" is the address of a null-ended string which is to be       located in the "vocabulary" given as a parameter. If the word        is not found, "addr2" is the string and "n" is false. If the       word is found, "addr2" is the compilation address and "n" is        set to one of two non-zero values. If the word found has the       immediate attribute set, "n" is one. If the word is non-immediate,        "n" is minus one. find ( addr1 -- addr2 n)       "addr1" is the address of a null-ended string. The string        contains a word name to located in the currently active       search order. If the word is not found, "addr2" is the       string and "n" is false. If the word is found, "addr2" is       the compilation address and "n" is set to one of two non-       zero values. If the word found has the immediate attribute        set, "n" is one. If the word is non-immediate, n is minus       one (true). forget ( -- )       Used in the form:               forget <name>       If <name> is found in the compilation vocabulary, delete       <name> from the dictionary and all words added to the        dictionary after <name> regardless of their vocabulary.        Failure to find <name> is an error condition. An error       condition also exists if the compilation vocabulary is        deleted.words ( -- ) immediate       Display the visible words in the current search chain.entry ( parameter mode code name -- )       Creates a new entry in the current definitions vocabulary       with the given arguments. The entry becomes the "last" entry        in the vocabulary.1forward ( -- )       Used in the following form to define a symbol which will       be defined later:               forward <name>       If the symbol is executed it will indirectly execute "abort".       When the symbol is later defined the forwarded symbol is       directed to the newly created symbol.: ( -- )       A defining word executed in the form:               : <name> <forth-definition> ;       Create a word definition for <name> in the compilation vocabulary       and set compilation state. The text from the input stream is       subsequently compiled. <name> is called a "colon definition".; ( -- ) immediate compilation       Stops compilation of a colon definitions, sets interpret state.       Additional actions may be taken for local variables and argument       frames and an exception block.create ( -- )       A defining word executed in the form:               create <name>       Create a dictionary entry for <name>. After <name> is created,        the next available dictionary location is the first byte of        <name>'s parameter field. When <name> is subsequently executed,        the address of the first byte of <name>'s parameter field is        left on the stack. "create" does not allocate space in <name>'s       parameter field.variable ( -- )       A defining word executed in the form:               variable <name>       A dictionary entry for <name> is created and four bytes are       allotted in its parameter field. This parameter field is to       be used for contents of the variable. The application is        responsible for initializing the contents of the variable       which is created. When <name> is later executed, the address       of its parameter field is placed on the stack. constant ( x -- )       A defining word executed in the form:               <value> constant <name>       Create a dictionary entry form <name> so that when <name> is        later executed, "value" will be left on the stack. vocabulary ( -- )       A defining word executed in the from:               vocabulary <name>       A dictionary entry for <name> is created which specifies a       new ordered list of word definitions. Subsequent execution       of <name> replaces the first vocabulary in the search order        with <name>. When <name> becomes the compilation vocabulary       new definitions will be appended to <name>'s list.       Demonstration of access of arguments passed to application.       Prints and executes an argument.field ( x -- )       Used in the form:               <offset> field <name>       When later <name> is used it will add the <offset> to the       top of the parameter stack.arc ( -- n)       Returns the number of arguments passed for the environment.       The first argument is always the name of the application:       "forth" or the name of the start symbol.argv ( n -- str)       Given an index returns the corresponding argument string.SEE ALSO       compiler(1), strings(1), queues(1), multi-tasking(1), memory(1)COPYING       Copyright (C) 1989 Mikael R.K. Patel       Permission is granted to make and distribute verbatim copies  